/**
\fn pdg::Animated::triggerEvent(string name)
\brief Emit a custom named animation event or record an event step.
\param name Custom nonempty name. Built-in animation event names and the eventType_ namespace are reserved.
\return This animated object for chaining.

During script recording, inside batch()/series(), or after andThen(), this records a zero-time step. Playback emits the event whenever execution reaches that step, including repeat visits. Otherwise it emits immediately on the selected animation. Subscribe with on(name, handler); custom events propagate to enclosing blocks. This does not post numeric PDG events. Invalid or reserved names raise an error before modifying the script. Portable snapshots preserve custom event names and playback cursors. Receivers may register handlers after restoration; events without handlers are harmless. Registered callback functions remain unsupported in portable snapshots.
\see on(), series(), andThen()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Animated::deleteScript(string name)
\brief Remove a definition and release its memory in the shared script library.

\param name Nonempty script name.
\return True if the definition existed and was removed; false otherwise.
This is a static method, also available on animated class constructors such as
Sprite, Camera, Part and Troupe. All class entry points use the same library.
Running instances continue using their published copies. Deleted names can be
redefined. Any retained recorder rejects further use; its definition graph is
released at deletion, while its small wrapper survives until garbage collection.
An empty name throws.

\code{.js}
pdg.Animated.defineScript('leave').moveBy(100, 0, 0.5).endScript();
sprite.playScript('leave');
pdg.Animated.deleteScript('leave'); // the running instance still finishes
\endcode
*/
